# Simple dynamic breadcrumb

Building navigation trails that adapt to the current file path is a classic requirement for static sites and simple CMS architectures. The goal here is straightforward: inspect the current URL structure and render a hierarchical list of links up to the root domain. In many frameworks, this logic is handled by route definitions or meta tags, but when working with plain PHP files served directly, you need a lightweight script that parses the path without heavy dependencies.

The core logic relies on splitting the full file path into segments and reconstructing them as navigation steps. Consider a file located at `website.com/templates/index.php`. The desired output is a two-step breadcrumb: "Website.com" linked to the domain root, followed by "Templates" as plain text. If the file deepens the hierarchy to `website.com/templates/template_some_name.php`, the trail extends to three steps, where both the domain and the first directory are clickable links, but the final segment remains static text.

To achieve this, you can implement a function that accepts the full path string and an optional base URL. The algorithm begins by normalizing the path. This means stripping any query parameters or fragments and ensuring the string starts with the protocol scheme if it isn't already present for display purposes. Once normalized, the path is split by the forward slash delimiter. This creates an array of segments like `['website.com', 'templates']` or `['website.com', 'templates', 'template_some_name.php']`.

The rendering loop iterates through these segments with index tracking. For each segment, you check if it matches the first element of the array. If it does, that segment represents the domain root and should be rendered as a full hyperlink pointing to the base URL. All subsequent segments are treated as directory or file names within the hierarchy and are displayed as plain text. The separator between these items is typically a greater-than symbol (`&gt;`).

Here is a practical implementation pattern using standard PHP functions:

```php
function renderBreadcrumb($currentPath, $baseUrl = '') {
    // Extract segments from the path, ignoring query strings
    $pathParts = array_filter(explode('/', basename(parse_url($currentPath, PHP_URL_PATH)) ?? ''));

    if (empty($pathParts) && strpos($currentPath, '?') !== false) {
         $pathParts = array_filter(explode('/', parse_url($currentPath, PHP_URL_PATH)));
    }

    // Handle root domain logic by preparsing the host
    $host = explode('.', $currentPath)[0]; 
    $segments = [];

    // Reconstruct a clean path structure for splitting
    $cleanPath = parse_url($currentPath, PHP_URL_PATH);
    $parts = array_filter(explode('/', trim($cleanPath, '/')));

    $html = '';
    $first = true;

    foreach ($parts as $index => $part) {
        if (empty($part)) continue;

        // Determine if this is the root domain or a sub-path
        if ($index === 0) {
            // Link to root
            $html .= '<a href="' . htmlspecialchars($baseUrl) . '">' . htmlspecialchars($part) . '</a>';
        } else {
            // Plain text
            $html .= ' &gt; ';
            $html .= htmlspecialchars($part);
        }

        if (!$first) {
            $html .= ' &gt; ';
        }
        $first = false;
    }

    return $html;
}
```

When integrating this into a template, pass the current request URI to the function. The logic assumes a flat file structure where directories map directly to URL segments. A significant trade-off with this approach is that it does not account for virtual hosts or complex routing rules common in modern PHP frameworks like Laravel. In those environments, you might prefer using the framework's built-in breadcrumbs package or generating meta tags for SEO rather than raw HTML output.

One potential pitfall is handling the final file segment. The example logic treats every segment after the root as a directory name, which works fine for folders but might display the filename (e.g., `index.php`) if you are viewing the script source directly. For production sites, you usually want to hide the `.php` extension in breadcrumbs unless it is part of a specific naming convention. You can modify the loop to strip file extensions from the last segment before outputting plain text.

If you are building a larger project and need consistent navigation logic across multiple views, consider centralizing this function in a base layout file or a utility library. This ensures that changes to breadcrumb styling or behavior propagate automatically throughout the application without requiring updates to every single view file.
